;;; An hrule's template writes a variable that no premise binds, and
;;; proposes nothing, in silence — M1f **W57**, register row
;;; `W57/hrule-assert-var` (`corpus/wrong_answers.toml`), filed by S1f.5.10
;;; (T1f.5.10.1, 2026-11-09), `open` and owned by S1f.5.10, on the user's
;;; ruling of 2026-11-07 on P1f.5's F13 (*"yes, file F13 with the
;;; refusal"*).
;;;
;;; ── What this program is ───────────────────────────────────────────────
;;;
;;; One fact `(p a)`, and an hrule, which the `(solve …)` block names, whose
;;; `:assert` writes `?y`, which its `:match` never binds.
;;;
;;; ── The answer, by hand ────────────────────────────────────────────────
;;;
;;; The ruling holds an hrule's `:assert` to the rule a rule's is held to
;;; (S1f.5.10's stage file): every variable bound on every way its `:match`
;;; can hold. `?y` is not, so the program is refused at load, and every
;;; command that loads it exits 1 with the loader's line (`ein test`: 2).
;;;
;;; ── What the engine answers ────────────────────────────────────────────
;;;
;;; The program loads, and the hrule's template builds no fact for any match,
;;; which the candidate generator skips (`hrule::Hrules::candidates`): H is
;;; empty and R is {∅}, exit 0 on every command, with no word of `?y` (`ein
;;; solve FILE` and `ein oracle FILE` at `2eb483e0`, release and debug
;;; alike).

(relation p Node :why "{?1} is a node")
(relation q Node :why "{?1} is a q")
(p a)
(hrule h ()
  :match (p ?x)
  :assert (q ?y)
  :why "suppose a q, since {?x} is a p")
(solve :hrules (h))
